Vorg the Sapient wrote:

> Don't know how difficult it would be to do though.  I notice
> fireproofing also protects against rot, which doesn't make much sense
> to me, so maybe all kinds of damage are treated the same?

They are. The commonly used term here for this generic proofing is
"fixed" (for example, a "fixed" robe is both fire- and rotproof, and a
fixed armour is both rust- and corrosionproof.

In technical terms, there is a "flag" attached to the data object in the
compuyter memory, which can be on or off, that controls whether an
object is proofed or not. There are no separate objects for fire,
corrosion, rot and rust, or for fixing a crysknife.

When wishing for an object, it is therefore sufficient to wish for a
"fixed" robe, shield, whatever. This is easy to remember and shortest to
type.

You don't have to know what an object is made of. Of course, this might
change in a new release... As it stands, wishing for a corrodeproof
elven dagger will give you a fireproof one, and a wish for a rotproof
sword will give you a rustproof one.

